WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:04.165
Lady Macbeth, along with all the guests, stared at her husband.

2
00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:11.095
A murmur was spreading through the hall. "Did he say ‘Banquo’s ghost ’?" whispered

3
00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:13.682
some. "He said Banquo should be

4
00:00:13.683 --> 00:00:17.349
dead!" "Who do you see, my lord?" Ross asked the

5
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:24.373
king. But Lady Macbeth quickly interrupted. "Please, such questions will only worsen his

6
00:00:24.374 --> 00:00:32.944
condition." Then, turning to all the guests she said, "I’m sorry that our evening has been spoiled," and she wished them all a kind

7
00:00:32.945 --> 00:00:37.413
goodnight. Attendants ushered the guests out of the hall, leaving Macbeth and his wife

8
00:00:37.414 --> 00:00:43.644
alone. "The ghost is gone; I am myself again," he reassured her. "Is it

9
00:00:43.645 --> 00:00:46.675
late?" Quietly, they began to talk about the

10
00:00:46.676 --> 00:00:54.098
evening. Lady Macbeth pointed out that Macduff had been absent. "He refused our invitation," she

11
00:00:54.099 --> 00:00:57.591
said. "It makes me suspect him," agreed

12
00:00:57.592 --> 00:01:03.638
Macbeth. Macbeth kept spies in the houses of all the Scottish noblemen, but he trusted Macduff the

13
00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:07.932
least. The more they talked, the more fears and suspicions grew in his

14
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:13.543
mind. Before he and Lady Macbeth went to bed, he resolved to visit the three witches once

15
00:01:13.544 --> 00:01:18.816
again. "I must know the worst," he said, "even by the worst

16
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:23.257
means." The witches, of course, knew that Macbeth was coming long before he

17
00:01:23.258 --> 00:01:29.424
arrived. In their dark cave, a huge cauldron bubbled over a fire. They circled around it,

18
00:01:29.425 --> 00:01:34.980
chanting: Round about the cauldron go; into it, poisoned entrails

19
00:01:34.981 --> 00:01:39.949
throw. Eye of newt and toe of frog, wool of bat and tongue of

20
00:01:39.951 --> 00:01:44.871
dog, Baboon’s blood and tail of snake, in the cauldron boil and

21
00:01:44.872 --> 00:01:49.889
bake. Double, double, toil and trouble; fire burn and cauldron

22
00:01:49.889 --> 00:01:54.411
bubble! Then, using a thick bone, they stirred the stinking pot

23
00:01:54.412 --> 00:02:01.728
together. Near the heath where he first saw the witches, a day’s journey from the palace, Macbeth found the entrance to their

24
00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:04.975
cave. A foul smell wafted from the dark

25
00:02:04.976 --> 00:02:10.395
opening. He covered his nose with a corner of his cloak and, ducking his head, stepped

26
00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:16.832
inside. An oily smoke burned his eyes, but he could see the three ugly witches in the red light of the cauldron’s

27
00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:21.363
fire. "What is it you do, you black and unholy hags?" he

28
00:02:21.364 --> 00:02:26.649
asked. "A deed without a name," said the first witch with a crooked

29
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.517
smile. "Come!" commanded the second

30
00:02:29.518 --> 00:02:32.785
witch. "We’ll answer you," said the

31
00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:39.395
third. Macbeth stepped closer and watched as the last witch dropped something red and wriggling into the bubbling

32
00:02:39.396 --> 00:02:44.919
liquid. A dark smoke rose, and Macbeth saw a ghostly head materialize in the

33
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.434
air. It wore a helmet, as if armed for

34
00:02:48.435 --> 00:02:54.005
war. "Tell me, you unknown power―" Macbeth began, but the first witch interrupted

35
00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:58.053
him. "He knows your thoughts. Listen and speak

36
00:02:58.054 --> 00:03:04.656
not." "Macbeth! Macbeth! Beware Macduff!" said the

37
00:03:04.657 --> 00:03:09.030
vision. "It knows my fear and cautions me well," said

38
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:14.331
Macbeth. "Just one word more—" but before he could ask, the vision descended into the

39
00:03:14.332 --> 00:03:20.212
pot. A second apparition rose from the cauldron. This one was in the form of a bloody

40
00:03:20.213 --> 00:03:30.054
child. "Macbeth! Macbeth! Laugh and scorn the power of men," it said, "for none born of woman shall harm

41
00:03:30.055 --> 00:03:33.212
Macbeth." Then it too descended into the creeping

42
00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:39.474
vapors. A third vision rose. This time it was a child wearing a crown and carrying a branch in its

43
00:03:39.475 --> 00:03:43.895
hand. It spoke: "Take no care, whoever your enemies

44
00:03:43.896 --> 00:03:50.498
are. Macbeth shall never be defeated until great Birnam Wood comes to high Dunsinane

45
00:03:50.499 --> 00:03:59.062
Hill." "That will never be!" Macbeth whispered excitedly. "How can the trees pull up their roots and march against my castle

46
00:03:59.063 --> 00:04:08.610
there? And ‘none born of woman’ shall harm me? Then, Macduff, I have no reason to fear you . . . But just in case, you will not live."

47
00:04:08.611 --> 00:04:12.046
He turned to look back at the cauldron, but the witches were gone.

48
00:04:12.047 --> 00:04:15.586
The fire was out and the cave was suddenly cold and desolate.

49
00:04:15.587 --> 00:04:22.959
Outside, the air was damp and fresh. Feeling as if he had awakened from a drugged sleep, Macbeth inhaled deeply.

50
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.443
On the long journey back to the palace, his thoughts churned.

51
00:04:26.444 --> 00:04:30.784
"They are the devil’s sisters, I’m sure, but they make me bold!

52
00:04:30.785 --> 00:04:35.418
From now on, the first thought of my heart will be the first act of my hand!

53
00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:41.628
I have waded so deep into a river of blood that to go back is just as far as to go on."

54
00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:48.608
His face grew grim. "‘Beware Macduff,’ they warned me. I must act on this advice!"

55
00:04:48.609 --> 00:04:52.283
When he arrived at the palace, a messenger was waiting for him.

56
00:04:52.284 --> 00:04:57.585
It was his spy from Macduff’s castle with news that Macduff had fled to England.

57
00:04:57.586 --> 00:05:02.545
Macbeth clenched his fists. "He has anticipated my plan!"

58
00:05:02.546 --> 00:05:10.013
In England, Duncan’s oldest son, Malcolm, had taken refuge at the court of the king, where many were sympathetic to his cause.

59
00:05:10.014 --> 00:05:15.688
Since the day Macbeth had come to power, the news from Scotland had grown steadily worse.

60
00:05:15.689 --> 00:05:21.410
The Scottish nobles feared him. And anyone who Macbeth suspected lived in terror of his army.

61
00:05:21.411 --> 00:05:26.378
Rumors of Macbeth’s murders were spreading. People whispered to each other about war.

62
00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:31.426
With the help of the English lords, Malcolm was slowly gathering an army of his own.

63
00:05:31.427 --> 00:05:36.592
The king of England had offered him ten thousand men, and more were joining every day.

64
00:05:36.593 --> 00:05:43.053
When Macduff arrived at the English court, he immediately offered to help the young prince take back his father’s throne.

65
00:05:43.054 --> 00:05:49.441
"Macbeth is a tyrant," said Macduff, as he strolled with Malcolm outside the English court.

66
00:05:49.442 --> 00:05:56.790
"Every morning, new widows mourn and new orphans cry. Scotland suffers badly under his rule."

67
00:05:56.791 --> 00:06:04.385
"Others hear our country’s cry," said Malcolm. "The English lords Siward and Northumberland have come to our side."

68
00:06:04.386 --> 00:06:10.600
Just then, a man approached from across the lawn, and they quickly recognized Ross, their fellow countryman.

69
00:06:10.601 --> 00:06:15.641
After they greeted each other, Macduff asked, "How do things stand in Scotland?"

70
00:06:15.642 --> 00:06:22.283
Ross shook his head. "Our homeland is becoming a graveyard. There are new atrocities every day."

71
00:06:22.284 --> 00:06:28.124
"How are my wife and children?" asked Macduff. "Macbeth has not assaulted their peace?"

72
00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:34.140
Ross paused. "I have words that I would rather howl in the desert than tell you.

73
00:06:34.141 --> 00:06:39.759
Fife Castle has been attacked, and your wife and children savagely slaughtered."

74
00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:46.226
"Merciful heaven!" exclaimed Malcolm, but Macduff was stunned and silent.

75
00:06:46.227 --> 00:06:52.821
There was a pause. "What, man! Give words to your grief," said Malcolm.

76
00:06:52.822 --> 00:06:55.842
"My children?" asked Macduff.

77
00:06:55.842 --> 00:07:02.544
"Wife, children, servants—all that Macbeth’s murderers found," said Ross.

78
00:07:02.545 --> 00:07:08.049
"Oh, torment and hell! My wife killed too?" cried Macduff.

79
00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.222
Ross nodded solemnly.

80
00:07:10.223 --> 00:07:14.168
"My children! Not all? Please, not all!"

81
00:07:14.168 --> 00:07:17.492
"You must bear it like a man," said Malcolm.

82
00:07:17.493 --> 00:07:23.612
Macduff turned to him angrily. "I shall do so! But I must feel it as a man, too.

83
00:07:23.613 --> 00:07:29.190
There was never anything so precious to me as my sweet family. Gone!"

84
00:07:29.191 --> 00:07:38.476
He turned from them and strode away. Then he turned back. "This grief is a whetstone to sharpen my sword!" he said.

85
00:07:38.477 --> 00:07:46.308
"I won’t let this sadness blunt my heart. Oh, I could fill my eyes with tears and cry sorrows with my tongue.

86
00:07:46.309 --> 00:07:55.617
But enough! Bring this demon of Scotland face to face with me, set him within reach of my sword, and heaven forgive me if he escapes death!"

